  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{\u2218})=2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={a}^{\u2218}+1\\,{440}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\\,x=1\\,{120}^{\u2218}+1\\,{440}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{\u2218})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{\u2218}={60}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n,\\,\\frac{x}{4}+{20}^{\u2218}={300}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{\u2218}+{60}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}=-{20}^{\u2218}+{300}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}={40}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n, & \\,\\frac{x}{4}={280}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n \\\\ x={160}^{\u2218}+1\\,{440}^{\u2218}\\cdot n, & \\,x=1\\,{120}^{\u2218}+1\\,{440}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=160.$",
